window.__programmeIndexChunk("passages",{"7595509ac5206ac88982eb9fe52873fdc7810642fe81b97978459488209fcaed":{"markdown":"## 7. The operator-valued coefficient-square formula\n\nSuppose $x:\\mathbb R\\to M$ is bounded, strongly star continuous and compactly supported. Its bounded integrated operator and coefficient square are\n$$\nX=\\int\\lambda(t)\\pi(x(t))\\,dt,\\qquad A=\\int x(t)^*x(t)\\,dt.\n\\tag{C27}\n$$\nThe first acts by vector integration and the second may be read ultraweakly; compact support and a uniform norm bound justify both. Substituting $u=s-t$ in the regular action gives\n$$\n(XD_q^*\\zeta_g)(s)=\\int e^{iqu}g(u)\\Delta^{-iu}x(s-u)\\xi\\,du.\n$$\nFor each $s$, this is the Fourier integral of an integrable square-integrable $H$-valued function. FF-2–3 and nonnegative scalar interchange, with the compact coefficient support and Gaussian bound, give\n\n<a id=\"FS-18\"><\/a><a id=\"mjx-eqn:FS-18\"><\/a>\n\n$$\n\\Phi(X^*X)\n=\\int |g(u)|^2\\int\\|\\Delta^{-iu}x(s-u)\\xi\\|^2\\,ds\\,du\n=\\int\\varphi(x(t)^*x(t))\\,dt.\n\\tag{C28}\n$$\nThe measure $dq/(2\\pi)$ accounts for the absence of a normalization factor in the displayed $u$-integral.\n\nLet $h=\\pi^{-1}(\\mathcal P(X^*X))$. Apply (C28) to $x(t)b$, for each $b\\in M$, and use the full sandwich identity (C20). The result is\n$$\n\\widehat\\varphi(b^*hb)=\\varphi(b^*Ab).\n\\tag{C29}\n$$\nIn the faithful state GNS representation, let $H_h$ be the finite-part positive operator of $h$. Its half-power form on $b\\xi$ is bounded by $\\|A\\|\\|b\\xi\\|^2$. This dense set first removes the infinite-part subspace. For a sequence $b_n\\xi\\to v$, the same bound applied to differences makes $H_h^{1/2}b_n\\xi$ Cauchy. Closedness gives $v\\in D(H_h^{1/2})$ and the bounded extension on every vector. Polarization of (C29) then gives $H_h=A$. Therefore\n\n<a id=\"FS-19\"><\/a><a id=\"mjx-eqn:FS-19\"><\/a>\n\n$$\n\\mathcal P(X^*X)=\\pi\\!\\left(\\int x(t)^*x(t)\\,dt\\right).\n\\tag{C30}\n$$\nAgreement on a dense set was used together with the closed-domain bound, not as an unsupported uniqueness assertion for unbounded operators.","html":"<h2>7. The operator-valued coefficient-square formula<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x:\\mathbb R\\to M\\)\">\\(x:\\mathbb R\\to M\\)<\/span> is bounded, strongly star continuous and compactly supported. Its bounded integrated operator and coefficient square are\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nX=\\int\\lambda(t)\\pi(x(t))\\,dt,\\qquad A=\\int x(t)^*x(t)\\,dt.\n\\tag{C27}\n\\]\">\\[\nX=\\int\\lambda(t)\\pi(x(t))\\,dt,\\qquad A=\\int x(t)^*x(t)\\,dt.\n\\tag{C27}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe first acts by vector integration and the second may be read ultraweakly; compact support and a uniform norm bound justify both. Substituting <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u=s-t\\)\">\\(u=s-t\\)<\/span> in the regular action gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n(XD_q^*\\zeta_g)(s)=\\int e^{iqu}g(u)\\Delta^{-iu}x(s-u)\\xi\\,du.\n\\]\">\\[\n(XD_q^*\\zeta_g)(s)=\\int e^{iqu}g(u)\\Delta^{-iu}x(s-u)\\xi\\,du.\n\\]<\/span>\nFor each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span>, this is the Fourier integral of an integrable square-integrable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>-valued function. FF-2–3 and nonnegative scalar interchange, with the compact coefficient support and Gaussian bound, give<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\Phi(X^*X)\n=\\int |g(u)|^2\\int\\|\\Delta^{-iu}x(s-u)\\xi\\|^2\\,ds\\,du\n=\\int\\varphi(x(t)^*x(t))\\,dt.\n\\tag{C28}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\Phi(X^*X)\n=\\int |g(u)|^2\\int\\|\\Delta^{-iu}x(s-u)\\xi\\|^2\\,ds\\,du\n=\\int\\varphi(x(t)^*x(t))\\,dt.\n\\tag{C28}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe measure <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(dq/(2\\pi)\\)\">\\(dq/(2\\pi)\\)<\/span> accounts for the absence of a normalization factor in the displayed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span>-integral.<\/p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=\\pi^{-1}(\\mathcal P(X^*X))\\)\">\\(h=\\pi^{-1}(\\mathcal P(X^*X))\\)<\/span>. Apply (C28) to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x(t)b\\)\">\\(x(t)b\\)<\/span>, for each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\in M\\)\">\\(b\\in M\\)<\/span>, and use the full sandwich identity (C20). The result is\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\widehat\\varphi(b^*hb)=\\varphi(b^*Ab).\n\\tag{C29}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\widehat\\varphi(b^*hb)=\\varphi(b^*Ab).\n\\tag{C29}\n\\]<\/span>\nIn the faithful state GNS representation, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_h\\)\">\\(H_h\\)<\/span> be the finite-part positive operator of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\)\">\\(h\\)<\/span>. Its half-power form on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\xi\\)\">\\(b\\xi\\)<\/span> is bounded by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|A\\|\\|b\\xi\\|^2\\)\">\\(\\|A\\|\\|b\\xi\\|^2\\)<\/span>. This dense set first removes the infinite-part subspace. For a sequence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b_n\\xi\\to v\\)\">\\(b_n\\xi\\to v\\)<\/span>, the same bound applied to differences makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_h^{1/2}b_n\\xi\\)\">\\(H_h^{1/2}b_n\\xi\\)<\/span> Cauchy. Closedness gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\in D(H_h^{1/2})\\)\">\\(v\\in D(H_h^{1/2})\\)<\/span> and the bounded extension on every vector. Polarization of (C29) then gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_h=A\\)\">\\(H_h=A\\)<\/span>. Therefore<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\mathcal P(X^*X)=\\pi\\!\\left(\\int x(t)^*x(t)\\,dt\\right).\n\\tag{C30}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\mathcal P(X^*X)=\\pi\\!\\left(\\int x(t)^*x(t)\\,dt\\right).\n\\tag{C30}\n\\]<\/span>\nAgreement on a dense set was used together with the closed-domain bound, not as an unsupported uniqueness assertion for unbounded operators.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L159.md","start_offset":26912,"end_offset":28806,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"75adea8ed1926eb8a52a7fd1b96d37dfcd31fcf4dfc4c64a1623f3fd6fba639e":{"markdown":"<a id=\"oa-flow.l127.cn5\"><\/a>\n## CN5. Split an abstract circle extension\n\nLet \\(L\\) be any LCA group containing a subgroup \\(C\\) topologically isomorphic to the circle. Compactness of \\(C\\) and the Hausdorff property make it closed. Choose an isomorphism \\(f:C\\to\\mathbb T\\). The full open-surjection portion of L118 DUAL-MAP extends the continuous character \\(f\\) to \\(g:L\\to\\mathbb T\\). The map \\(\\rho=f^{-1}g\\) is a continuous retraction onto \\(C\\). Its closed kernel \\(C'\\) gives the factorization\n\n<a id=\"equation-cn16\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\nL\\longrightarrow C\\times C',\\quad\nx\\longmapsto\\bigl(\\rho(x),x-\\rho(x)\\bigr)\n\\tag{CN16}\n\\]\n\nIts continuous inverse is \\((c,c')\\mapsto c+c'\\). This supplies the entire topological circle-summand argument, with no countable character-extension theorem.\n\nNow let \\(J\\) be any discrete abelian group and let \\(\\lambda:J^2\\to\\mathbb T\\) be a normalized symmetric two-cocycle. On \\(E=\\mathbb T\\times J\\) use\n\\((a,j)(b,k)=(ab\\lambda(j,k),j+k)\\).\nThe cocycle identity proves associativity by comparing the two scalar coefficients in a triple product; normalization gives identity \\((1,0)\\). Both inverse products equal the identity for\n\\((a^{-1}\\lambda(j,-j)^{-1},-j)\\);\nsymmetry makes the displayed product commutative. The group operations are continuous because \\(J\\) is discrete and the scalar operations are continuous on each open fiber. The product is Hausdorff, and each circle fiber is an open compact neighborhood, so \\(E\\) is LCA.\n\nSplit its closed circle subgroup by the preceding retraction. The second-coordinate quotient restricts to a bijection \\(C'\\to J\\): each element factors uniquely into a circle element and a complement element, and the intersection of the two subgroups is trivial. Its complement is discrete, because its intersection with each open circle fiber is a singleton. Thus this restriction and its inverse are continuous. Write the inverse section as \\(j\\mapsto(c(j),j)\\). Multiplying two section values gives\n\n<a id=\"equation-cn17\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\nc(j)c(k)\\lambda(j,k)=c(j+k).\n\\tag{CN17}\n\\]\n\nIt follows that \\(c(j)v_j\\) is an exact representation whenever the original projective family exists. For our particular family in \\(D\\), CN3 explicitly supplies \\(c(j)=\\overline{\\chi(v_j)}\\). The abstract splitting theorem remains useful when a symmetric scalar cocycle was not initially obtained from operators in such an algebra.","html":"<h2>CN5. Split an abstract circle extension<\/h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span> be any LCA group containing a subgroup <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span> topologically isomorphic to the circle. Compactness of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span> and the Hausdorff property make it closed. Choose an isomorphism <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f:C\\to\\mathbb T\\)\">\\(f:C\\to\\mathbb T\\)<\/span>. The full open-surjection portion of L118 DUAL-MAP extends the continuous character <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g:L\\to\\mathbb T\\)\">\\(g:L\\to\\mathbb T\\)<\/span>. The map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho=f^{-1}g\\)\">\\(\\rho=f^{-1}g\\)<\/span> is a continuous retraction onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span>. Its closed kernel <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C&#x27;\\)\">\\(C&#x27;\\)<\/span> gives the factorization<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nL\\longrightarrow C\\times C&#x27;,\\quad\nx\\longmapsto\\bigl(\\rho(x),x-\\rho(x)\\bigr)\n\\tag{CN16}\n\\]\">\\[\nL\\longrightarrow C\\times C&#x27;,\\quad\nx\\longmapsto\\bigl(\\rho(x),x-\\rho(x)\\bigr)\n\\tag{CN16}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Its continuous inverse is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((c,c&#x27;)\\mapsto c+c&#x27;\\)\">\\((c,c&#x27;)\\mapsto c+c&#x27;\\)<\/span>. This supplies the entire topological circle-summand argument, with no countable character-extension theorem.<\/p>\n<p>Now let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J\\)\">\\(J\\)<\/span> be any discrete abelian group and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda:J^2\\to\\mathbb T\\)\">\\(\\lambda:J^2\\to\\mathbb T\\)<\/span> be a normalized symmetric two-cocycle. On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E=\\mathbb T\\times J\\)\">\\(E=\\mathbb T\\times J\\)<\/span> use\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((a,j)(b,k)=(ab\\lambda(j,k),j+k)\\)\">\\((a,j)(b,k)=(ab\\lambda(j,k),j+k)\\)<\/span>.\nThe cocycle identity proves associativity by comparing the two scalar coefficients in a triple product; normalization gives identity <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((1,0)\\)\">\\((1,0)\\)<\/span>. Both inverse products equal the identity for\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((a^{-1}\\lambda(j,-j)^{-1},-j)\\)\">\\((a^{-1}\\lambda(j,-j)^{-1},-j)\\)<\/span>;\nsymmetry makes the displayed product commutative. The group operations are continuous because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J\\)\">\\(J\\)<\/span> is discrete and the scalar operations are continuous on each open fiber. The product is Hausdorff, and each circle fiber is an open compact neighborhood, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> is LCA.<\/p>\n<p>Split its closed circle subgroup by the preceding retraction. The second-coordinate quotient restricts to a bijection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C&#x27;\\to J\\)\">\\(C&#x27;\\to J\\)<\/span>: each element factors uniquely into a circle element and a complement element, and the intersection of the two subgroups is trivial. Its complement is discrete, because its intersection with each open circle fiber is a singleton. Thus this restriction and its inverse are continuous. Write the inverse section as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\mapsto(c(j),j)\\)\">\\(j\\mapsto(c(j),j)\\)<\/span>. Multiplying two section values gives<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nc(j)c(k)\\lambda(j,k)=c(j+k).\n\\tag{CN17}\n\\]\">\\[\nc(j)c(k)\\lambda(j,k)=c(j+k).\n\\tag{CN17}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>It follows that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c(j)v_j\\)\">\\(c(j)v_j\\)<\/span> is an exact representation whenever the original projective family exists. For our particular family in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>, CN3 explicitly supplies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c(j)=\\overline{\\chi(v_j)}\\)\">\\(c(j)=\\overline{\\chi(v_j)}\\)<\/span>. The abstract splitting theorem remains useful when a symmetric scalar cocycle was not initially obtained from operators in such an algebra.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L127.md","start_offset":15873,"end_offset":18252,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"75ddc3b0b498e675c1a37636f7cea58ccd1e69f27ea29b45e388d9843a0fddbd":{"markdown":"<a id=\"oa-flow.orbits.reference\"><\/a>\n## Reference probabilities in the Haar class\n\nChoose a Borel \\(w:G\\to(0,\\infty)\\) with \\(\\int_Gw\\,dg=1\\). Such a function exists: partition a countable compact exhaustion into finite-Haar-measure Borel pieces and give the \\(n\\)-th piece value \\(2^{-n}/(1+m(E_n))\\), then normalize. Haar measure is nonzero, so the normalizing integral is positive.\n\nPut \\(\\nu=q_*\\lambda\\), regarding \\(\\lambda\\) as a probability on \\(X_1\\), and define\n\\[\n \\eta_y(D)=\\int_Gw(g)1_D(gs(y))\\,dg,\\qquad\n \\mu_0(D)=\\int_Y\\eta_y(D)\\,d\\nu(y).\n \\tag{K7}\n\\]\nThe integrands are jointly Borel, since the action and \\(s\\) are Borel. The parameter-integration result above applies. Thus \\(\\eta\\) is a probability kernel. Each \\(\\eta_y\\) is concentrated on \\(q^{-1}(y)\\), and \\(q_*\\mu_0=\\nu\\).\n\nFor \\(x\\in X_1\\), also set\n\\[\n \\kappa_x(D)=\\int_Gw(g)1_D(gx)\\,dg,\\qquad\n \\bar\\lambda(D)=\\int_{X_1}\\kappa_x(D)\\,d\\lambda(x)\n              =\\int_Gw(g)\\lambda(g^{-1}D)\\,dg.\n \\tag{K8}\n\\]\nQuasi-invariance gives \\(\\bar\\lambda\\sim\\lambda\\). One implication follows because every translate of a \\(\\lambda\\)-null set is null. For the other, if \\(\\bar\\lambda(D)=0\\), then \\(\\lambda(g^{-1}D)=0\\) for Haar-almost every \\(g\\). At least one such \\(g\\) exists, and quasi-invariance implies \\(\\lambda(D)=0\\). Moreover \\(q(gx)=q(x)\\) gives \\(q_*\\bar\\lambda=\\nu\\), so smearing preserves the quotient distribution.\n\nFor every \\(x\\), choose merely for this argument one \\(h\\) with \\(x=hs(q(x))\\). No measurable choice of \\(h\\) is needed. Since \\(w>0\\) everywhere and right translation preserves the Haar null ideal,\n\\[\n \\kappa_x(D)=0\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n \\eta_{q(x)}(D)=0\n \\qquad(D\\text{ Borel}).\n \\tag{K9}\n\\]\nFor each fixed \\(D\\), integrate this equivalence. It gives\n\\[\n \\bar\\lambda(D)=0\n \\Longleftrightarrow \\eta_{q(x)}(D)=0\\ \\lambda\\text{-a.e. }x\n \\Longleftrightarrow \\eta_y(D)=0\\ \\nu\\text{-a.e. }y\n \\Longleftrightarrow \\mu_0(D)=0.\n \\tag{K10}\n\\]\nTherefore \\(\\mu_0\\sim\\lambda\\). This argument is before, and independent of, any conditional measure construction. It does not intersect uncountably many exceptional sets.","html":"<h2>Reference probabilities in the Haar class<\/h2>\n<p>Choose a Borel <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w:G\\to(0,\\infty)\\)\">\\(w:G\\to(0,\\infty)\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int_Gw\\,dg=1\\)\">\\(\\int_Gw\\,dg=1\\)<\/span>. Such a function exists: partition a countable compact exhaustion into finite-Haar-measure Borel pieces and give the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>-th piece value <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2^{-n}/(1+m(E_n))\\)\">\\(2^{-n}/(1+m(E_n))\\)<\/span>, then normalize. Haar measure is nonzero, so the normalizing integral is positive.<\/p>\n<p>Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu=q_*\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\nu=q_*\\lambda\\)<\/span>, regarding <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\lambda\\)<\/span> as a probability on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X_1\\)\">\\(X_1\\)<\/span>, and define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\eta_y(D)=\\int_Gw(g)1_D(gs(y))\\,dg,\\qquad\n \\mu_0(D)=\\int_Y\\eta_y(D)\\,d\\nu(y).\n \\tag{K7}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\eta_y(D)=\\int_Gw(g)1_D(gs(y))\\,dg,\\qquad\n \\mu_0(D)=\\int_Y\\eta_y(D)\\,d\\nu(y).\n \\tag{K7}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe integrands are jointly Borel, since the action and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span> are Borel. The parameter-integration result above applies. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta\\)\">\\(\\eta\\)<\/span> is a probability kernel. Each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta_y\\)\">\\(\\eta_y\\)<\/span> is concentrated on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q^{-1}(y)\\)\">\\(q^{-1}(y)\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q_*\\mu_0=\\nu\\)\">\\(q_*\\mu_0=\\nu\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in X_1\\)\">\\(x\\in X_1\\)<\/span>, also set\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\kappa_x(D)=\\int_Gw(g)1_D(gx)\\,dg,\\qquad\n \\bar\\lambda(D)=\\int_{X_1}\\kappa_x(D)\\,d\\lambda(x)\n              =\\int_Gw(g)\\lambda(g^{-1}D)\\,dg.\n \\tag{K8}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\kappa_x(D)=\\int_Gw(g)1_D(gx)\\,dg,\\qquad\n \\bar\\lambda(D)=\\int_{X_1}\\kappa_x(D)\\,d\\lambda(x)\n              =\\int_Gw(g)\\lambda(g^{-1}D)\\,dg.\n \\tag{K8}\n\\]<\/span>\nQuasi-invariance gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bar\\lambda\\sim\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\bar\\lambda\\sim\\lambda\\)<\/span>. One implication follows because every translate of a <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\lambda\\)<\/span>-null set is null. For the other, if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bar\\lambda(D)=0\\)\">\\(\\bar\\lambda(D)=0\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda(g^{-1}D)=0\\)\">\\(\\lambda(g^{-1}D)=0\\)<\/span> for Haar-almost every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span>. At least one such <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span> exists, and quasi-invariance implies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda(D)=0\\)\">\\(\\lambda(D)=0\\)<\/span>. Moreover <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q(gx)=q(x)\\)\">\\(q(gx)=q(x)\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q_*\\bar\\lambda=\\nu\\)\">\\(q_*\\bar\\lambda=\\nu\\)<\/span>, so smearing preserves the quotient distribution.<\/p>\n<p>For every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>, choose merely for this argument one <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\)\">\\(h\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=hs(q(x))\\)\">\\(x=hs(q(x))\\)<\/span>. No measurable choice of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\)\">\\(h\\)<\/span> is needed. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w&gt;0\\)\">\\(w&gt;0\\)<\/span> everywhere and right translation preserves the Haar null ideal,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\kappa_x(D)=0\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n \\eta_{q(x)}(D)=0\n \\qquad(D\\text{ Borel}).\n \\tag{K9}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\kappa_x(D)=0\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n \\eta_{q(x)}(D)=0\n \\qquad(D\\text{ Borel}).\n \\tag{K9}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor each fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>, integrate this equivalence. It gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\bar\\lambda(D)=0\n \\Longleftrightarrow \\eta_{q(x)}(D)=0\\ \\lambda\\text{-a.e. }x\n \\Longleftrightarrow \\eta_y(D)=0\\ \\nu\\text{-a.e. }y\n \\Longleftrightarrow \\mu_0(D)=0.\n \\tag{K10}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\bar\\lambda(D)=0\n \\Longleftrightarrow \\eta_{q(x)}(D)=0\\ \\lambda\\text{-a.e. }x\n \\Longleftrightarrow \\eta_y(D)=0\\ \\nu\\text{-a.e. }y\n \\Longleftrightarrow \\mu_0(D)=0.\n \\tag{K10}\n\\]<\/span>\nTherefore <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu_0\\sim\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\mu_0\\sim\\lambda\\)<\/span>. This argument is before, and independent of, any conditional measure construction. It does not intersect uncountably many exceptional sets.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L69.md","start_offset":12273,"end_offset":14385,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"755275e7d8500dd9bd4d5df21710a0d447a2a5e4c2aa2a0c6bce7106381e9bdd":{"markdown":"<a id=\"ds-5\"><\/a>\n## 5. Compare faithful weights under pointwise scalar absorption\n\nLet \\(\\chi_1,\\chi_2\\) be faithful normal semifinite weights on \\(M\\), each with properly infinite centralizer, and assume \\(\\chi_j\\sim c\\chi_j\\) for every \\(c>0\\). Faithfulness turns the equivalences into unitaries. Taking the desired scalar or its reciprocal gives, for every \\(r\\), some unitary \\(u\\) with\n\\[\n \\chi_j\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}(u)=e^r\\chi_j.\n \\tag{DSEL23}\n\\]\nThe exact inner formula [GDA34](OA-FLOW-GDA.md#equation-gda34) and the scalar derivative formula [BC24](OA-FLOW-BC.md#bc-5) show that this equality is equivalent to\n\\[\n u^*\\sigma_t^{\\chi_j}(u)=e^{irt}1,\n \\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n \\sigma_t^{\\chi_j}(u)=e^{irt}u.\n \\tag{DSEL24}\n\\]\nFor the converse use [GDA7's fixed-reference injectivity](OA-FLOW-GDA.md#gda-7), so this is an equivalence of weights with their full scalar normalization. The selection lemma supplies Borel fields \\(X_j(r)\\) satisfying (DSEL24) for all \\(r,t\\).\n\nSet\n\\[\n A=M\\bar\\otimes B(L),\\qquad\n \\Omega_j=\\chi_j\\bar\\otimes\\operatorname{Tr},\\qquad\n k=1\\otimes e^Q,\n \\quad(Qf)(q)=qf(q),\n \\qquad \\eta_j=(\\Omega_j)_k.\n \\tag{DSEL25}\n\\]\nThe usual scalar trace is normalized to take value one on a rank-one projection. [TW2](OA-FLOW-TW.md#tw-2) constructs \\(\\Omega_j\\) on the whole cone; [TW5](OA-FLOW-TW.md#tw-5) gives\n\\(\\sigma_t^{\\Omega_j}=\\sigma_t^{\\chi_j}\\bar\\otimes\\operatorname{id}\\). The positive nonsingular density \\(k\\) is affiliated with this centralizer. The full [CZ2 construction](OA-FLOW-CZ.md#cz-2) is\n\\[\n \\eta_j(Y)=\\sup_{\\delta>0}\n  \\Omega_j(k_\\delta^{1/2}Yk_\\delta^{1/2}),\n \\qquad k_\\delta=k(1+\\delta k)^{-1},\n \\tag{DSEL26}\n\\]\nwith the increasing scalar supremum as \\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\), and [the derivative of a centralizer density](OA-FLOW-CZ.md#cz-6) gives\n\\((D\\eta_j:D\\Omega_j)_t=1\\otimes V_t\\). The weights \\(\\eta_j\\) are faithful normal semifinite.\n\nFor the Borel multipliers \\(\\mathfrak X_j\\) constructed above, (DSEL22) now reads\n\\[\n \\mathfrak X_j^*\\sigma_t^{\\Omega_j}(\\mathfrak X_j)\n =1\\otimes V_t.\n \\tag{DSEL27}\n\\]\nThe inner derivative formula and fixed-reference injectivity imply\n\\[\n \\Omega_j\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}(\\mathfrak X_j)=\\eta_j\n \\quad\\text{on }A_+.\n \\tag{DSEL28}\n\\]\nThis is the step for which mere unrelated pointwise choices would not suffice.\n\nThe unconditional regularized comparison in [regularized weight comparison](OA-FLOW-DWC.md#dwc-2) applies to the two faithful weights and gives\n\\[\n \\eta_2=\\eta_1\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}(v^*)\n \\quad\\text{for a unitary }v\\in A.\n \\tag{DSEL29}\n\\]\nIts proof uses the strongly continuous relative modular cocycle\n\\(c_t=(D\\chi_2:D\\chi_1)_t\\), rather than continuity of \\(X_j\\). Specifically [DWC1](OA-FLOW-DWC.md#dwc-1) constructs \\(v\\) with\n\\[\n c_t\\otimes V_t\n =v(1\\otimes V_t)\n       (\\sigma_t^{\\chi_1}\\bar\\otimes\\operatorname{id})(v^*);\n \\tag{DSEL30}\n\\]\nDWC2 then identifies the actual weights by the normalized derivative. Combining (DSEL28) and (DSEL29), in order, gives\n\\[\n U=\\mathfrak X_1v^*\\mathfrak X_2^*\\in\\mathcal U(A),\n \\qquad \\Omega_2=\\Omega_1\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}(U).\n \\tag{DSEL31}\n\\]\n\nFor completeness, the cancellation uses no remaining regularity of the selected fields. Choose, by [properly infinite filling families](OA-FLOW-PC.md#oa-flow.pc.5), filling isometries \\(r_{jn}\\in M_{\\chi_j}\\), with\n\\(r_{jn}^*r_{jm}=\\delta_{nm}1\\) and \\(\\sum_nr_{jn}r_{jn}^*=1\\) strongly. In a countable orthonormal basis of \\(L\\), let\n\\[\n R_j=\\sum_{n\\ge1}r_{jn}\\otimes e_{1n},\\qquad\n e=1\\otimes e_{11}.\n \\tag{DSEL32}\n\\]\nThe row series and its adjoint converge strongly by orthogonal square-sum tails; hence \\(R_j\\in A_{\\Omega_j}\\), with \\(R_j^*R_j=1\\), \\(R_jR_j^*=e\\). The [whole-cone row identity in DWC3](OA-FLOW-DWC.md#dwc-3) gives\n\\[\n \\Omega_j(R_jYR_j^*)=\\Omega_j(Y),\\qquad\n \\Omega_j(R_j^*ZR_j)=\\Omega_j(Z)\\quad(Z\\in(eAe)_+).\n \\tag{DSEL33}\n\\]\nTo recall why infinity is included, centralizer ideal stability and cyclicity prove equality when either side is finite; applying the inverse corner map proves finiteness in the other direction. Thus one side cannot be finite when the other is infinite. No finite value of \\(\\Omega_j(e)\\) is assumed.\n\nNow \\(a=R_1UR_2^*\\) is a unitary of \\(eAe\\), and\n\\[\n \\Omega_1(aZa^*)\n =\\Omega_1(UR_2^*ZR_2U^*)\n =\\Omega_2(R_2^*ZR_2)\n =\\Omega_2(Z).\n \\tag{DSEL34}\n\\]\nWrite \\(a=w\\otimes e_{11}\\). The rank-one normalization\n\\(\\Omega_j(x\\otimes e_{11})=\\chi_j(x)\\) gives\n\\[\n \\boxed{\\ \\chi_2(x)=\\chi_1(wxw^*)\\quad(x\\in M_+),\n          \\qquad w\\in\\mathcal U(M).\\ }\n \\tag{DSEL35}\n\\]\nThis proves faithful dominant-weight uniqueness from the original pointwise scalar hypothesis.","html":"<h2>5. Compare faithful weights under pointwise scalar absorption<\/h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi_1,\\chi_2\\)\">\\(\\chi_1,\\chi_2\\)<\/span> be faithful normal semifinite weights on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, each with properly infinite centralizer, and assume <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi_j\\sim c\\chi_j\\)\">\\(\\chi_j\\sim c\\chi_j\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c&gt;0\\)\">\\(c&gt;0\\)<\/span>. Faithfulness turns the equivalences into unitaries. Taking the desired scalar or its reciprocal gives, for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span>, some unitary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span> with\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\chi_j\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}(u)=e^r\\chi_j.\n \\tag{DSEL23}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\chi_j\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}(u)=e^r\\chi_j.\n \\tag{DSEL23}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe exact inner formula <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GDA.html#equation-gda34\">GDA34<\/a> and the scalar derivative formula <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-BC.html#bc-5\">BC24<\/a> show that this equality is equivalent to\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n u^*\\sigma_t^{\\chi_j}(u)=e^{irt}1,\n \\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n \\sigma_t^{\\chi_j}(u)=e^{irt}u.\n \\tag{DSEL24}\n\\]\">\\[\n u^*\\sigma_t^{\\chi_j}(u)=e^{irt}1,\n \\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n \\sigma_t^{\\chi_j}(u)=e^{irt}u.\n \\tag{DSEL24}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor the converse use <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GDA.html#gda-7\">GDA7's fixed-reference injectivity<\/a>, so this is an equivalence of weights with their full scalar normalization. The selection lemma supplies Borel fields <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X_j(r)\\)\">\\(X_j(r)\\)<\/span> satisfying (DSEL24) for all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r,t\\)\">\\(r,t\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Set\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n A=M\\bar\\otimes B(L),\\qquad\n \\Omega_j=\\chi_j\\bar\\otimes\\operatorname{Tr},\\qquad\n k=1\\otimes e^Q,\n \\quad(Qf)(q)=qf(q),\n \\qquad \\eta_j=(\\Omega_j)_k.\n \\tag{DSEL25}\n\\]\">\\[\n A=M\\bar\\otimes B(L),\\qquad\n \\Omega_j=\\chi_j\\bar\\otimes\\operatorname{Tr},\\qquad\n k=1\\otimes e^Q,\n \\quad(Qf)(q)=qf(q),\n \\qquad \\eta_j=(\\Omega_j)_k.\n \\tag{DSEL25}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe usual scalar trace is normalized to take value one on a rank-one projection. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-TW.html#tw-2\">TW2<\/a> constructs <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_j\\)\">\\(\\Omega_j\\)<\/span> on the whole cone; <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-TW.html#tw-5\">TW5<\/a> gives\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma_t^{\\Omega_j}=\\sigma_t^{\\chi_j}\\bar\\otimes\\operatorname{id}\\)\">\\(\\sigma_t^{\\Omega_j}=\\sigma_t^{\\chi_j}\\bar\\otimes\\operatorname{id}\\)<\/span>. The positive nonsingular density <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span> is affiliated with this centralizer. The full <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CZ.html#cz-2\">CZ2 construction<\/a> is\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\eta_j(Y)=\\sup_{\\delta&gt;0}\n  \\Omega_j(k_\\delta^{1/2}Yk_\\delta^{1/2}),\n \\qquad k_\\delta=k(1+\\delta k)^{-1},\n \\tag{DSEL26}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\eta_j(Y)=\\sup_{\\delta&gt;0}\n  \\Omega_j(k_\\delta^{1/2}Yk_\\delta^{1/2}),\n \\qquad k_\\delta=k(1+\\delta k)^{-1},\n \\tag{DSEL26}\n\\]<\/span>\nwith the increasing scalar supremum as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\)\">\\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\)<\/span>, and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CZ.html#cz-6\">the derivative of a centralizer density<\/a> gives\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((D\\eta_j:D\\Omega_j)_t=1\\otimes V_t\\)\">\\((D\\eta_j:D\\Omega_j)_t=1\\otimes V_t\\)<\/span>. The weights <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta_j\\)\">\\(\\eta_j\\)<\/span> are faithful normal semifinite.<\/p>\n<p>For the Borel multipliers <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathfrak X_j\\)\">\\(\\mathfrak X_j\\)<\/span> constructed above, (DSEL22) now reads\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\mathfrak X_j^*\\sigma_t^{\\Omega_j}(\\mathfrak X_j)\n =1\\otimes V_t.\n \\tag{DSEL27}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\mathfrak X_j^*\\sigma_t^{\\Omega_j}(\\mathfrak X_j)\n =1\\otimes V_t.\n \\tag{DSEL27}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe inner derivative formula and fixed-reference injectivity imply\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\Omega_j\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}(\\mathfrak X_j)=\\eta_j\n \\quad\\text{on }A_+.\n \\tag{DSEL28}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\Omega_j\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}(\\mathfrak X_j)=\\eta_j\n \\quad\\text{on }A_+.\n \\tag{DSEL28}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis is the step for which mere unrelated pointwise choices would not suffice.<\/p>\n<p>The unconditional regularized comparison in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-DWC.html#dwc-2\">regularized weight comparison<\/a> applies to the two faithful weights and gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\eta_2=\\eta_1\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}(v^*)\n \\quad\\text{for a unitary }v\\in A.\n \\tag{DSEL29}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\eta_2=\\eta_1\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}(v^*)\n \\quad\\text{for a unitary }v\\in A.\n \\tag{DSEL29}\n\\]<\/span>\nIts proof uses the strongly continuous relative modular cocycle\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_t=(D\\chi_2:D\\chi_1)_t\\)\">\\(c_t=(D\\chi_2:D\\chi_1)_t\\)<\/span>, rather than continuity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X_j\\)\">\\(X_j\\)<\/span>. Specifically <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-DWC.html#dwc-1\">DWC1<\/a> constructs <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span> with\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n c_t\\otimes V_t\n =v(1\\otimes V_t)\n       (\\sigma_t^{\\chi_1}\\bar\\otimes\\operatorname{id})(v^*);\n \\tag{DSEL30}\n\\]\">\\[\n c_t\\otimes V_t\n =v(1\\otimes V_t)\n       (\\sigma_t^{\\chi_1}\\bar\\otimes\\operatorname{id})(v^*);\n \\tag{DSEL30}\n\\]<\/span>\nDWC2 then identifies the actual weights by the normalized derivative. Combining (DSEL28) and (DSEL29), in order, gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n U=\\mathfrak X_1v^*\\mathfrak X_2^*\\in\\mathcal U(A),\n \\qquad \\Omega_2=\\Omega_1\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}(U).\n \\tag{DSEL31}\n\\]\">\\[\n U=\\mathfrak X_1v^*\\mathfrak X_2^*\\in\\mathcal U(A),\n \\qquad \\Omega_2=\\Omega_1\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}(U).\n \\tag{DSEL31}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>For completeness, the cancellation uses no remaining regularity of the selected fields. Choose, by <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PC.html#oa-flow.pc.5\">properly infinite filling families<\/a>, filling isometries <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r_{jn}\\in M_{\\chi_j}\\)\">\\(r_{jn}\\in M_{\\chi_j}\\)<\/span>, with\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r_{jn}^*r_{jm}=\\delta_{nm}1\\)\">\\(r_{jn}^*r_{jm}=\\delta_{nm}1\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_nr_{jn}r_{jn}^*=1\\)\">\\(\\sum_nr_{jn}r_{jn}^*=1\\)<\/span> strongly. In a countable orthonormal basis of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span>, let\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n R_j=\\sum_{n\\ge1}r_{jn}\\otimes e_{1n},\\qquad\n e=1\\otimes e_{11}.\n \\tag{DSEL32}\n\\]\">\\[\n R_j=\\sum_{n\\ge1}r_{jn}\\otimes e_{1n},\\qquad\n e=1\\otimes e_{11}.\n \\tag{DSEL32}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe row series and its adjoint converge strongly by orthogonal square-sum tails; hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R_j\\in A_{\\Omega_j}\\)\">\\(R_j\\in A_{\\Omega_j}\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R_j^*R_j=1\\)\">\\(R_j^*R_j=1\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R_jR_j^*=e\\)\">\\(R_jR_j^*=e\\)<\/span>. The <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-DWC.html#dwc-3\">whole-cone row identity in DWC3<\/a> gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\Omega_j(R_jYR_j^*)=\\Omega_j(Y),\\qquad\n \\Omega_j(R_j^*ZR_j)=\\Omega_j(Z)\\quad(Z\\in(eAe)_+).\n \\tag{DSEL33}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\Omega_j(R_jYR_j^*)=\\Omega_j(Y),\\qquad\n \\Omega_j(R_j^*ZR_j)=\\Omega_j(Z)\\quad(Z\\in(eAe)_+).\n \\tag{DSEL33}\n\\]<\/span>\nTo recall why infinity is included, centralizer ideal stability and cyclicity prove equality when either side is finite; applying the inverse corner map proves finiteness in the other direction. Thus one side cannot be finite when the other is infinite. No finite value of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_j(e)\\)\">\\(\\Omega_j(e)\\)<\/span> is assumed.<\/p>\n<p>Now <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a=R_1UR_2^*\\)\">\\(a=R_1UR_2^*\\)<\/span> is a unitary of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(eAe\\)\">\\(eAe\\)<\/span>, and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\Omega_1(aZa^*)\n =\\Omega_1(UR_2^*ZR_2U^*)\n =\\Omega_2(R_2^*ZR_2)\n =\\Omega_2(Z).\n \\tag{DSEL34}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\Omega_1(aZa^*)\n =\\Omega_1(UR_2^*ZR_2U^*)\n =\\Omega_2(R_2^*ZR_2)\n =\\Omega_2(Z).\n \\tag{DSEL34}\n\\]<\/span>\nWrite <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a=w\\otimes e_{11}\\)\">\\(a=w\\otimes e_{11}\\)<\/span>. The rank-one normalization\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_j(x\\otimes e_{11})=\\chi_j(x)\\)\">\\(\\Omega_j(x\\otimes e_{11})=\\chi_j(x)\\)<\/span> gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\boxed{\\ \\chi_2(x)=\\chi_1(wxw^*)\\quad(x\\in M_+),\n          \\qquad w\\in\\mathcal U(M).\\ }\n \\tag{DSEL35}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\boxed{\\ \\chi_2(x)=\\chi_1(wxw^*)\\quad(x\\in M_+),\n          \\qquad w\\in\\mathcal U(M).\\ }\n \\tag{DSEL35}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis proves faithful dominant-weight uniqueness from the original pointwise scalar hypothesis.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-DS.md","start_offset":16102,"end_offset":20692,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"757810b888ba486812d790a56c4337b9a949f322492a8d0b7a4bb18ff66b8eca":{"markdown":"<a id=\"cs-diagnostics\"><\/a>\n## 6. Two exact diagnostics\n\n**A nonconstant central phase.** Use the actual translation decomposition\n\\[\n N=B(\\ell^2)\\bar\\otimes L^\\infty(\\mathbb R,dz),\\qquad\n \\alpha_s f(z)=f(z+s),\\qquad\n \\tau_N(x)=\\int e^z\\operatorname{Tr}(x(z))\\,dz .\n\\]\n[OES7](OA-FLOW-OES.md#oes-7) constructs this whole crossed product and its normalized dual weight. In (CS14), write \\(z\\) for the center coordinate and \\(q\\) for the independent spectral coordinate. For \\(a(z)=e^{i\\omega z^2}\\),\n\\[\n c_s(z)=a(z)\\overline{a(z+s)}\n       =e^{-i\\omega(2zs+s^2)},\\qquad\n b_\\varphi(c)(z,q)=e^{i\\omega(2zq-q^2)}.\n \\tag{CS27}\n\\]\nThe dual flow sends \\((z,q)\\) to \\((z+s,q+s)\\). Subtracting these two quadratic phases gives \\(-\\omega(2zs+s^2)\\), exactly \\(\\delta_-b=c_s\\). Conjugation by \\(b_\\varphi(c^*)\\) multiplies the crossing shift by \\(c_s(z-q)\\), the coefficient embedding required in (CS17).\n\n**Same cocycle, opposite boundary.** For \\(c_s=e^{its}\\), \\(b_\\varphi(c)=h_\\varphi^{it}\\) solves \\(\\delta_-b=c\\). Its adjoint solves \\(\\delta_+b=c\\). Keeping \\(b_\\varphi(c)\\) unchanged while reversing the boundary would replace \\(t\\) by \\(-t\\); (CS5) and (CS11) detect the error.\n\n![The independent joint coordinate and its diagonal obstruction](../assets/canonical-cocycle-sections/canonical-section.svg)\n\n*Figure 2.* The left panel is the exact phase in (CS27); the arrow is the dual translation \\((z,q)\\mapsto(z+s,q+s)\\). The right panel shows the decreasing strips used in the normality obstruction after (CS26). Their diagonal restrictions stay one although their product-measure limit is zero. Neither panel substitutes a numerical test for the proofs. [Source](../assets/canonical-cocycle-sections/render.py), [diagnostics](../assets/canonical-cocycle-sections/diagnostics.json), and [terms](../assets/canonical-cocycle-sections/TERMS.md).","html":"<h2>6. Two exact diagnostics<\/h2>\n<p><strong>A nonconstant central phase.<\/strong> Use the actual translation decomposition\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n N=B(\\ell^2)\\bar\\otimes L^\\infty(\\mathbb R,dz),\\qquad\n \\alpha_s f(z)=f(z+s),\\qquad\n \\tau_N(x)=\\int e^z\\operatorname{Tr}(x(z))\\,dz .\n\\]\">\\[\n N=B(\\ell^2)\\bar\\otimes L^\\infty(\\mathbb R,dz),\\qquad\n \\alpha_s f(z)=f(z+s),\\qquad\n \\tau_N(x)=\\int e^z\\operatorname{Tr}(x(z))\\,dz .\n\\]<\/span>\n<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-OES.html#oes-7\">OES7<\/a> constructs this whole crossed product and its normalized dual weight. In (CS14), write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span> for the center coordinate and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\)\">\\(q\\)<\/span> for the independent spectral coordinate. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a(z)=e^{i\\omega z^2}\\)\">\\(a(z)=e^{i\\omega z^2}\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n c_s(z)=a(z)\\overline{a(z+s)}\n       =e^{-i\\omega(2zs+s^2)},\\qquad\n b_\\varphi(c)(z,q)=e^{i\\omega(2zq-q^2)}.\n \\tag{CS27}\n\\]\">\\[\n c_s(z)=a(z)\\overline{a(z+s)}\n       =e^{-i\\omega(2zs+s^2)},\\qquad\n b_\\varphi(c)(z,q)=e^{i\\omega(2zq-q^2)}.\n \\tag{CS27}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe dual flow sends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((z,q)\\)\">\\((z,q)\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((z+s,q+s)\\)\">\\((z+s,q+s)\\)<\/span>. Subtracting these two quadratic phases gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-\\omega(2zs+s^2)\\)\">\\(-\\omega(2zs+s^2)\\)<\/span>, exactly <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta_-b=c_s\\)\">\\(\\delta_-b=c_s\\)<\/span>. Conjugation by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b_\\varphi(c^*)\\)\">\\(b_\\varphi(c^*)\\)<\/span> multiplies the crossing shift by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_s(z-q)\\)\">\\(c_s(z-q)\\)<\/span>, the coefficient embedding required in (CS17).<\/p>\n<p><strong>Same cocycle, opposite boundary.<\/strong> For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_s=e^{its}\\)\">\\(c_s=e^{its}\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b_\\varphi(c)=h_\\varphi^{it}\\)\">\\(b_\\varphi(c)=h_\\varphi^{it}\\)<\/span> solves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta_-b=c\\)\">\\(\\delta_-b=c\\)<\/span>. Its adjoint solves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta_+b=c\\)\">\\(\\delta_+b=c\\)<\/span>. Keeping <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b_\\varphi(c)\\)\">\\(b_\\varphi(c)\\)<\/span> unchanged while reversing the boundary would replace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-t\\)\">\\(-t\\)<\/span>; (CS5) and (CS11) detect the error.<\/p>\n<p><a class=\"figure-link\" href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/canonical-cocycle-sections/canonical-section.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/canonical-cocycle-sections/canonical-section.svg\" alt=\"The independent joint coordinate and its diagonal obstruction\" /><span>Open figure at full size<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<p><em>Figure 2.<\/em> The left panel is the exact phase in (CS27); the arrow is the dual translation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((z,q)\\mapsto(z+s,q+s)\\)\">\\((z,q)\\mapsto(z+s,q+s)\\)<\/span>. The right panel shows the decreasing strips used in the normality obstruction after (CS26). Their diagonal restrictions stay one although their product-measure limit is zero. Neither panel substitutes a numerical test for the proofs. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/canonical-cocycle-sections/render.py\">Source<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/canonical-cocycle-sections/diagnostics.json\">diagnostics<\/a>, and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/canonical-cocycle-sections/TERMS.md\">terms<\/a>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-CSEC.md","start_offset":19541,"end_offset":21381,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"751f612ff2c93e5756b2e460f4fe9b9ae39db1576186710ee1e05ba9aaa859d6":{"markdown":"<a id=\"nnr-boundary\"><\/a>\n## 7. Nonamenability and the precise surviving boundary\n\nThe matrices\n\\[\n A_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix},\n \\qquad B_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\2&1\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\ngenerate a free group of rank two in \\(\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\). In projective coordinate \\(r=x/y\\), let \\(D_A=\\{|r|>1\\}\\), including infinity, and \\(D_B=\\{|r|<1\\}\\). For every nonzero integer \\(k\\), \\(A_0^k\\) sends \\(D_B\\) into \\(D_A\\). Also \\(B_0^k\\) sends \\(D_A\\) into \\(D_B\\): its formula is \\(r/(2kr+1)\\), and \\(|2kr+1|>|r|\\) when \\(|r|>1\\); infinity goes to \\(1/(2k)\\).\n\nFor a reduced alternating word whose first and last powers have the same generator, applying the word to the other domain sends that domain into the first generator's domain. Hence the word is not the identity. If the first and last generators differ, conjugate by a suitable nonzero power of the first, avoiding cancellation of its initial exponent. The resulting reduced word starts and ends with that generator. A nonzero power of one generator is directly nonidentity from its matrix. Therefore every nonempty reduced word is nonidentity, proving freeness.\n\n[TRP (TR32)](OA-FLOW-TRP.md#trp-obstruction) proves by the four first-letter sets that \\(F_2\\) has no invariant mean. To pass from \\(N\\) to this subgroup \\(H\\), choose one representative \\(r\\) of each left \\(H\\)-orbit \\(Hr\\) in \\(N\\). Extend \\(f\\in\\ell^\\infty(H)\\) by \\(\\widetilde f(hr)=f(h)\\). This map is unital, positive and intertwines left \\(H\\)-translations. Composing an invariant mean on \\(N\\) with this map would give one on \\(H\\), a contradiction. Thus \\(N\\) is nonamenable.\n\nNonamenability alone does not prohibit an inner kernel on an AFD factor of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\). For any countable \\(G\\) and normal \\(N\\), put \\(Q=G/N\\). [TRP Section 8](OA-FLOW-TRP.md#trp-bernoulli) constructs an AFD tensor factor \\(P_Q\\) of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) over \\(Q\\times\\mathbb N_{>0}\\), with an action \\(\\beta\\) of \\(Q\\) outer at every nonidentity coset, including finite and trivial \\(Q\\). Set \\(\\alpha_g=\\beta_{gN}\\). Its inner kernel is exactly \\(N\\). Choosing \\(u_n=1\\) makes both characteristic functions one. Thus the trivial class is realizable for arbitrary \\(N\\), including the subgroup in Theorem 3.\n\nThe obstruction requires inner implementers for the whole \\(\\Gamma\\). The same irrational translation multiplier is realizable when the prescribed inner subgroup is only the amenable \\(T=\\mathbb Z^2\\) inside the ambient \\(\\Gamma\\). Restrict (NNR2) to translations:\n\\[\n \\mu_T(v,w)=e^{\\pi i\\sqrt2\\,\\omega(v,w)},\\qquad\n \\lambda_T(v,(z,A))=e^{2\\pi i\\sqrt2\\,\\omega(z,v)}.\n\\]\nThese are the restriction of the compatible extension and action already constructed. Inverse conjugation sends \\(v\\) to \\(A^{-1}v\\), and the ratio (NNR3) gives the displayed \\(\\lambda_T\\). They satisfy CPP, and ARP gives an AFD action of \\(\\Gamma\\) on a factor of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) with exact inner kernel \\(T\\). Its \\(\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\) automorphisms need not be inner and therefore supply no projective lift of all \\(\\Gamma\\) inside the factor. This identifies the hypothesis responsible for the contradiction.\n\nThe quantifiers and conclusions are:\n\n- For arbitrary countable ambient \\(G\\), amenable \\(N\\), and every normalized compatible pair, the finite-subgroup or ARP regular construction gives an AFD realization of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\).\n- For arbitrary \\(N\\) and the trivial class, the quotient Bernoulli construction gives an AFD realization of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) with exact inner kernel.\n- For the explicit nonamenable \\(N\\) and irrational pair of Theorem 3, no AFD realization of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) exists.\n- This proof does not characterize all realizable compatible classes for arbitrary nonamenable \\(N\\). CPP, TRP, NB and ARP do not provide that characterization either. The unrestricted positive assertion is refuted by the counterexample.\n\nThese quantifiers cannot be interchanged. A change of factor type, group labels, twist class or normal-subgroup scope would change the original realization question.","html":"<h2>7. Nonamenability and the precise surviving boundary<\/h2>\n<p>The matrices\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n A_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;2\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix},\n \\qquad B_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\2&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\">\\[\n A_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;2\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix},\n \\qquad B_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\2&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]<\/span>\ngenerate a free group of rank two in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\)<\/span>. In projective coordinate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r=x/y\\)\">\\(r=x/y\\)<\/span>, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_A=\\{|r|&gt;1\\}\\)\">\\(D_A=\\{|r|&gt;1\\}\\)<\/span>, including infinity, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_B=\\{|r|&lt;1\\}\\)\">\\(D_B=\\{|r|&lt;1\\}\\)<\/span>. For every nonzero integer <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A_0^k\\)\">\\(A_0^k\\)<\/span> sends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_B\\)\">\\(D_B\\)<\/span> into <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_A\\)\">\\(D_A\\)<\/span>. Also <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B_0^k\\)\">\\(B_0^k\\)<\/span> sends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_A\\)\">\\(D_A\\)<\/span> into <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_B\\)\">\\(D_B\\)<\/span>: its formula is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r/(2kr+1)\\)\">\\(r/(2kr+1)\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|2kr+1|&gt;|r|\\)\">\\(|2kr+1|&gt;|r|\\)<\/span> when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|r|&gt;1\\)\">\\(|r|&gt;1\\)<\/span>; infinity goes to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/(2k)\\)\">\\(1/(2k)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For a reduced alternating word whose first and last powers have the same generator, applying the word to the other domain sends that domain into the first generator's domain. Hence the word is not the identity. If the first and last generators differ, conjugate by a suitable nonzero power of the first, avoiding cancellation of its initial exponent. The resulting reduced word starts and ends with that generator. A nonzero power of one generator is directly nonidentity from its matrix. Therefore every nonempty reduced word is nonidentity, proving freeness.<\/p>\n<p><a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-TRP.html#trp-obstruction\">TRP (TR32)<\/a> proves by the four first-letter sets that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_2\\)\">\\(F_2\\)<\/span> has no invariant mean. To pass from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> to this subgroup <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>, choose one representative <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span> of each left <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>-orbit <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Hr\\)\">\\(Hr\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>. Extend <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\in\\ell^\\infty(H)\\)\">\\(f\\in\\ell^\\infty(H)\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widetilde f(hr)=f(h)\\)\">\\(\\widetilde f(hr)=f(h)\\)<\/span>. This map is unital, positive and intertwines left <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>-translations. Composing an invariant mean on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> with this map would give one on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>, a contradiction. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> is nonamenable.<\/p>\n<p>Nonamenability alone does not prohibit an inner kernel on an AFD factor of type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)<\/span>. For any countable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> and normal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q=G/N\\)\">\\(Q=G/N\\)<\/span>. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-TRP.html#trp-bernoulli\">TRP Section 8<\/a> constructs an AFD tensor factor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_Q\\)\">\\(P_Q\\)<\/span> of type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)<\/span> over <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q\\times\\mathbb N_{&gt;0}\\)\">\\(Q\\times\\mathbb N_{&gt;0}\\)<\/span>, with an action <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta\\)\">\\(\\beta\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q\\)\">\\(Q\\)<\/span> outer at every nonidentity coset, including finite and trivial <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q\\)\">\\(Q\\)<\/span>. Set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_g=\\beta_{gN}\\)\">\\(\\alpha_g=\\beta_{gN}\\)<\/span>. Its inner kernel is exactly <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>. Choosing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_n=1\\)\">\\(u_n=1\\)<\/span> makes both characteristic functions one. Thus the trivial class is realizable for arbitrary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, including the subgroup in Theorem 3.<\/p>\n<p>The obstruction requires inner implementers for the whole <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span>. The same irrational translation multiplier is realizable when the prescribed inner subgroup is only the amenable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T=\\mathbb Z^2\\)\">\\(T=\\mathbb Z^2\\)<\/span> inside the ambient <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span>. Restrict (NNR2) to translations:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\mu_T(v,w)=e^{\\pi i\\sqrt2\\,\\omega(v,w)},\\qquad\n \\lambda_T(v,(z,A))=e^{2\\pi i\\sqrt2\\,\\omega(z,v)}.\n\\]\">\\[\n \\mu_T(v,w)=e^{\\pi i\\sqrt2\\,\\omega(v,w)},\\qquad\n \\lambda_T(v,(z,A))=e^{2\\pi i\\sqrt2\\,\\omega(z,v)}.\n\\]<\/span>\nThese are the restriction of the compatible extension and action already constructed. Inverse conjugation sends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A^{-1}v\\)\">\\(A^{-1}v\\)<\/span>, and the ratio (NNR3) gives the displayed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda_T\\)\">\\(\\lambda_T\\)<\/span>. They satisfy CPP, and ARP gives an AFD action of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span> on a factor of type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)<\/span> with exact inner kernel <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span>. Its <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\)<\/span> automorphisms need not be inner and therefore supply no projective lift of all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span> inside the factor. This identifies the hypothesis responsible for the contradiction.<\/p>\n<p>The quantifiers and conclusions are:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>For arbitrary countable ambient <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span>, amenable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, and every normalized compatible pair, the finite-subgroup or ARP regular construction gives an AFD realization of type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>For arbitrary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> and the trivial class, the quotient Bernoulli construction gives an AFD realization of type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)<\/span> with exact inner kernel.<\/li>\n<li>For the explicit nonamenable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> and irrational pair of Theorem 3, no AFD realization of type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)<\/span> exists.<\/li>\n<li>This proof does not characterize all realizable compatible classes for arbitrary nonamenable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>. CPP, TRP, NB and ARP do not provide that characterization either. The unrestricted positive assertion is refuted by the counterexample.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>These quantifiers cannot be interchanged. A change of factor type, group labels, twist class or normal-subgroup scope would change the original realization question.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-NNR.md","start_offset":27563,"end_offset":31624,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"759343c75f2265da54768d909f6932635a7838d19a793402f84740d47a1a12e3":{"markdown":"<a id=\"l128-fc8\"><\/a>\n\n## FC8. When a finite covariant pair recovers the regular algebra\n\nLet a finite group \\(G\\) act normally on a nonzero von Neumann algebra \\(M\\). Assume that each \\(\\alpha_s\\), \\(s\\ne e\\), is free in the precise sense of [FC3](#l128-fc3):\n\\[\n ca=\\alpha_s(a)c\\quad\\text{for all }a\\in M\n \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad c=0.\n \\tag{FC8.a}\n\\]\nLet \\(\\rho:M\\to B(K)\\) be faithful, normal and unital, and let \\(V\\) be a unitary representation of \\(G\\) satisfying\n\\(V_s\\rho(a)V_s^*=\\rho(\\alpha_s(a))\\). The Hilbert space \\(K\\) may be arbitrary. Then the natural generator correspondence is a normal star isomorphism from \\(N=M\\rtimes_\\alpha G\\) onto \\(\\{\\rho(M),V(G)\\}''\\).\n\nTo prove this, use the expectation of [FC1](#l128-fc1) and put \\(x_s=E(xu_s^*)\\). FC0's coefficient uniqueness proves, because the sum is finite,\n\\[\n x=\\sum_{s\\in G}\\pi(x_s)u_s,\n \\qquad F(x)=\\sum_{s\\in G}\\rho(x_s)V_s\n \\quad(x\\in N).\n \\tag{FC8.b}\n\\]\nThe arithmetic of [FC2A](#l128-fc2a) specializes to\n\\[\n (xy)_r=\\sum_{s\\in G}x_s\\alpha_s(y_{s^{-1}r}),\\qquad\n (x^*)_r=\\alpha_r(x_{r^{-1}}^*).\n \\tag{FC8.c}\n\\]\nCovariance makes the two finite formulas for \\(F(x)F(y)\\) and \\(F(xy)\\) identical, and likewise for adjoints; \\(F(1)=1\\). Every coefficient compression, multiplication by a fixed unitary and \\(\\rho\\) is normal, so \\(F\\) is a normal star homomorphism on all of \\(N\\).\n\nThe ultraweakly closed kernel is a two-sided star ideal. The support-projection argument of [NCF5](OA-FLOW-NCF.md#ncf-5), applied to this representation of \\(N\\), gives \\(\\ker F=Nz\\) for a central projection \\(z\\in N\\). In particular \\(z\\) commutes with \\(\\pi(M)\\). [FC4](#l128-fc4) and (FC8.a) imply \\(z=\\pi(c)\\) for a central projection \\(c\\in M\\). But\n\\(0=F(z)=\\rho(c)\\), so coefficient faithfulness makes \\(z=0\\). Thus \\(F\\) is injective.\n\nThe faithful C*-norm argument in [ST2](OA-FLOW-ST12.md#oa-flow.st.2) makes \\(F\\) isometric. Its range \\(B=F(N)\\) is consequently a norm-closed unital C*-algebra. By [ST1](OA-FLOW-ST12.md#oa-flow.st.1), the unit ball of \\(N\\) is ultraweakly compact; normality makes its image the compact, hence closed, unit ball of \\(B\\). For a contraction in \\(B''\\), [BD4](OA-FLOW-BD.md#oa-flow.bd.4) supplies contractions in \\(B\\) converging strongly-star. The uniform vector-series tail estimate of [BD5](OA-FLOW-BD.md#oa-flow.bd.5) makes this convergence ultraweak. The compact image ball therefore contains the contraction, proving \\(B=B''\\). This uses a deliberately bounded approximating net; it makes no boundedness claim about an arbitrary convergent net.\n\nThe finite formula puts \\(B\\) inside \\(\\{\\rho(M),V(G)\\}''\\), while the generator values and the just-proved von Neumann closedness give the reverse inclusion. For the inverse's normality on the whole algebra, first note that \\(F\\) is a homeomorphism of the ultraweak unit balls, by compactness and the Hausdorff topology. If \\(\\omega\\in N_*\\), the bounded linear functional \\(\\omega\\circ F^{-1}\\) is continuous on the unit ball of \\(B\\). ST1's linear criterion puts it in the actual concrete predual \\(B_*\\). Doing this for every \\(\\omega\\) proves ultraweak continuity of \\(F^{-1}\\) on all of \\(B\\), exactly as in ST2. The theorem therefore includes the complete normal inverse, not just continuity on one ball.\n\nBoth hypotheses distinguishing this statement from a mere covariance assertion matter. For the outerness condition, let \\(G=\\{e,s\\}\\) have order two, take \\(M=\\mathbb C^3\\), and let \\(\\alpha_s\\) fix the first coordinate and exchange the other two. It is non-inner, since all inner automorphisms of an abelian algebra are the identity, but it is inner on the invariant first central summand. On \\(K=\\mathbb C^3\\), take the faithful diagonal representation and\n\\[\n V_s=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\\\0&0&1\\\\0&1&0\\end{pmatrix},\n \\qquad p=(1,0,0).\n \\tag{FC8.d}\n\\]\nThis is a covariant unitary representation. Since \\(\\rho(p)V_s=\\rho(p)\\), the natural finite homomorphism kills \\(\\pi(p)(1-u_s)\\). That element is nonzero: its identity and \\(s\\)-coefficients are \\(p\\) and \\(-p\\), by FC0. Plain non-innerness therefore does not suffice on a nonfactor.\n\nFaithfulness of \\(\\rho\\) is independent of that obstruction. Already for the trivial group, the normal unital coordinate quotient \\(\\mathbb C^2\\to\\mathbb C\\) cannot give an isomorphism with the original coefficient algebra. A nonfaithful covariant coefficient representation instead has an invariant central kernel, and NCF5 identifies the faithful supported quotient algebra. The discarded central summand is not recovered. The zero algebra has its separate unique zero-map interpretation.","html":"<h2>FC8. When a finite covariant pair recovers the regular algebra<\/h2>\n<p>Let a finite group <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> act normally on a nonzero von Neumann algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>. Assume that each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_s\\)\">\\(\\alpha_s\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\ne e\\)\">\\(s\\ne e\\)<\/span>, is free in the precise sense of <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#l128-fc3\">FC3<\/a>:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n ca=\\alpha_s(a)c\\quad\\text{for all }a\\in M\n \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad c=0.\n \\tag{FC8.a}\n\\]\">\\[\n ca=\\alpha_s(a)c\\quad\\text{for all }a\\in M\n \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad c=0.\n \\tag{FC8.a}\n\\]<\/span>\nLet <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho:M\\to B(K)\\)\">\\(\\rho:M\\to B(K)\\)<\/span> be faithful, normal and unital, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\)\">\\(V\\)<\/span> be a unitary representation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> satisfying\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V_s\\rho(a)V_s^*=\\rho(\\alpha_s(a))\\)\">\\(V_s\\rho(a)V_s^*=\\rho(\\alpha_s(a))\\)<\/span>. The Hilbert space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> may be arbitrary. Then the natural generator correspondence is a normal star isomorphism from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N=M\\rtimes_\\alpha G\\)\">\\(N=M\\rtimes_\\alpha G\\)<\/span> onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{\\rho(M),V(G)\\}&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\{\\rho(M),V(G)\\}&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>To prove this, use the expectation of <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#l128-fc1\">FC1<\/a> and put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x_s=E(xu_s^*)\\)\">\\(x_s=E(xu_s^*)\\)<\/span>. FC0's coefficient uniqueness proves, because the sum is finite,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n x=\\sum_{s\\in G}\\pi(x_s)u_s,\n \\qquad F(x)=\\sum_{s\\in G}\\rho(x_s)V_s\n \\quad(x\\in N).\n \\tag{FC8.b}\n\\]\">\\[\n x=\\sum_{s\\in G}\\pi(x_s)u_s,\n \\qquad F(x)=\\sum_{s\\in G}\\rho(x_s)V_s\n \\quad(x\\in N).\n \\tag{FC8.b}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe arithmetic of <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#l128-fc2a\">FC2A<\/a> specializes to\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (xy)_r=\\sum_{s\\in G}x_s\\alpha_s(y_{s^{-1}r}),\\qquad\n (x^*)_r=\\alpha_r(x_{r^{-1}}^*).\n \\tag{FC8.c}\n\\]\">\\[\n (xy)_r=\\sum_{s\\in G}x_s\\alpha_s(y_{s^{-1}r}),\\qquad\n (x^*)_r=\\alpha_r(x_{r^{-1}}^*).\n \\tag{FC8.c}\n\\]<\/span>\nCovariance makes the two finite formulas for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(x)F(y)\\)\">\\(F(x)F(y)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(xy)\\)\">\\(F(xy)\\)<\/span> identical, and likewise for adjoints; <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(1)=1\\)\">\\(F(1)=1\\)<\/span>. Every coefficient compression, multiplication by a fixed unitary and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> is normal, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span> is a normal star homomorphism on all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The ultraweakly closed kernel is a two-sided star ideal. The support-projection argument of <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-NCF.html#ncf-5\">NCF5<\/a>, applied to this representation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ker F=Nz\\)\">\\(\\ker F=Nz\\)<\/span> for a central projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\in N\\)\">\\(z\\in N\\)<\/span>. In particular <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span> commutes with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(M)\\)\">\\(\\pi(M)\\)<\/span>. <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#l128-fc4\">FC4<\/a> and (FC8.a) imply <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z=\\pi(c)\\)\">\\(z=\\pi(c)\\)<\/span> for a central projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\in M\\)\">\\(c\\in M\\)<\/span>. But\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0=F(z)=\\rho(c)\\)\">\\(0=F(z)=\\rho(c)\\)<\/span>, so coefficient faithfulness makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z=0\\)\">\\(z=0\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span> is injective.<\/p>\n<p>The faithful C*-norm argument in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-ST12.html#oa-flow.st.2\">ST2<\/a> makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span> isometric. Its range <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B=F(N)\\)\">\\(B=F(N)\\)<\/span> is consequently a norm-closed unital C*-algebra. By <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-ST12.html#oa-flow.st.1\">ST1<\/a>, the unit ball of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> is ultraweakly compact; normality makes its image the compact, hence closed, unit ball of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>. For a contraction in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(B&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-BD.html#oa-flow.bd.4\">BD4<\/a> supplies contractions in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span> converging strongly-star. The uniform vector-series tail estimate of <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-BD.html#oa-flow.bd.5\">BD5<\/a> makes this convergence ultraweak. The compact image ball therefore contains the contraction, proving <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B=B&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(B=B&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span>. This uses a deliberately bounded approximating net; it makes no boundedness claim about an arbitrary convergent net.<\/p>\n<p>The finite formula puts <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span> inside <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{\\rho(M),V(G)\\}&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\{\\rho(M),V(G)\\}&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span>, while the generator values and the just-proved von Neumann closedness give the reverse inclusion. For the inverse's normality on the whole algebra, first note that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span> is a homeomorphism of the ultraweak unit balls, by compactness and the Hausdorff topology. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\in N_*\\)\">\\(\\omega\\in N_*\\)<\/span>, the bounded linear functional <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\circ F^{-1}\\)\">\\(\\omega\\circ F^{-1}\\)<\/span> is continuous on the unit ball of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>. ST1's linear criterion puts it in the actual concrete predual <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B_*\\)\">\\(B_*\\)<\/span>. Doing this for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\)\">\\(\\omega\\)<\/span> proves ultraweak continuity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F^{-1}\\)\">\\(F^{-1}\\)<\/span> on all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>, exactly as in ST2. The theorem therefore includes the complete normal inverse, not just continuity on one ball.<\/p>\n<p>Both hypotheses distinguishing this statement from a mere covariance assertion matter. For the outerness condition, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G=\\{e,s\\}\\)\">\\(G=\\{e,s\\}\\)<\/span> have order two, take <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M=\\mathbb C^3\\)\">\\(M=\\mathbb C^3\\)<\/span>, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_s\\)\">\\(\\alpha_s\\)<\/span> fix the first coordinate and exchange the other two. It is non-inner, since all inner automorphisms of an abelian algebra are the identity, but it is inner on the invariant first central summand. On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K=\\mathbb C^3\\)\">\\(K=\\mathbb C^3\\)<\/span>, take the faithful diagonal representation and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n V_s=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0&amp;0\\\\0&amp;0&amp;1\\\\0&amp;1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix},\n \\qquad p=(1,0,0).\n \\tag{FC8.d}\n\\]\">\\[\n V_s=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0&amp;0\\\\0&amp;0&amp;1\\\\0&amp;1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix},\n \\qquad p=(1,0,0).\n \\tag{FC8.d}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis is a covariant unitary representation. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(p)V_s=\\rho(p)\\)\">\\(\\rho(p)V_s=\\rho(p)\\)<\/span>, the natural finite homomorphism kills <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(p)(1-u_s)\\)\">\\(\\pi(p)(1-u_s)\\)<\/span>. That element is nonzero: its identity and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span>-coefficients are <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-p\\)\">\\(-p\\)<\/span>, by FC0. Plain non-innerness therefore does not suffice on a nonfactor.<\/p>\n<p>Faithfulness of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> is independent of that obstruction. Already for the trivial group, the normal unital coordinate quotient <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb C^2\\to\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(\\mathbb C^2\\to\\mathbb C\\)<\/span> cannot give an isomorphism with the original coefficient algebra. A nonfaithful covariant coefficient representation instead has an invariant central kernel, and NCF5 identifies the faithful supported quotient algebra. The discarded central summand is not recovered. The zero algebra has its separate unique zero-map interpretation.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L128.md","start_offset":116878,"end_offset":121469,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"}});
